\[
\sum_{k=0}^{p^r-1}(2k+\alpha)\frac{(\alpha)_k^3\left(\tfrac12\right)_k}{(1)_k^3\left(\tfrac12+\alpha\right)_k}
\equiv \alpha^{\ast_r}p^r-\frac{(\alpha^{\ast_r})^3}{\left(\tfrac12+\alpha\right)^{\ast_r}}\,p^{r+2}H_{\alpha^{\ast_r}p-\alpha^{\ast_{r-1}}}^{(2)}
\pmod{p^{r+3}}.
\]
Among the choices, this exact statement is not listed. Choice (E) has the same right-hand side but only modulo \(p^{r+2}\), so it is weaker than the actual theorem. Therefore the best choice is that one of the listed options is true, but a stronger result holds.

\boxed{C}

The main theorem for this mixed local/nonlocal \(p\)-equation gives a quantitative interior Hölder estimate on the smaller ball \(B_{R/2}(\xi_0)\), with constant depending on \((Q,p,s,\gamma)\), and the nonlocal contribution enters through the standard \((p-1,sp)\)-tail, i.e. with the factor \(R^{sp}\).

So the correct estimate is
\[
\sup_{\xi\ne\eta\in B_{R/2}(\xi_0)}
\frac{|u(\xi)-u(\eta)|}{\|\eta^{-1}\circ\xi\|_{\mathbb H^n}^{\gamma}}
\le \frac{c}{R^{\gamma}}
\left(\|u\|_{L^\infty(B_R(\xi_0))}
+\operatorname{Tail}_{p-1,sp,sp}(u;\xi_0,R)\right),
\]
with \(c=c(Q,p,s,\gamma)>0\).

